+## TLS certificate verification
+
+You can optionally verify your database's TLS certificate and authenticate with a client certificate. These fields appear in the source form below the **Verify the server certificate?** toggle:
+
+- **Verify the server certificate?** — Check that your database's TLS certificate comes from a trusted authority. Defaults to **No**. A self-signed certificate or a private authority passes only when you add its certificate authority below. When connecting through an SSH tunnel, PostHog checks the certificate chain but not the hostname, because the tunnel presents your database on a local address.
+- **Certificate authority (optional)** — The PEM certificate of the authority that signed your database's certificate. Add it when your database uses a private authority or a self-signed certificate. Adding a certificate authority automatically turns on certificate verification even if the toggle is set to **No**.
+- **Client certificate (optional)** — A PEM certificate that PostHog presents to your database. Add it when your database requires mutual TLS (mTLS) authentication.
+- **Client key (optional)** — The private key of the client certificate, in PEM format and without a passphrase. This is a secret field — API responses do not include it.
+
+A source that sets none of these fields connects with the same TLS behavior as before.
+
+<CalloutBox icon="IconInfo" title="Client certificate requirements" type="fyi">
+
+- A client certificate requires both the certificate and its private key. If one is missing, the credential check fails with an error.
+- The client key must not have a passphrase. An encrypted key fails the credential check.
+- A client certificate requires TLS even with verification off — MySQL refuses a server that offers no TLS when a client certificate is set, so the certificate is never silently skipped.
+- To turn off client certificate authentication, clear the client certificate field. The stored key is ignored when no certificate is set.
+
+</CalloutBox>
